    "result_text": "The build is green \u2014 the only test (`AcceptanceTest`) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` rule on `POST /api/owners`, following the existing pattern used for the other derived, creation-time owner fields (`customerCode`, `namesakeCount`, etc.):\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only boolean `bulkSignupWarning` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it automatically.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `bulkSignupWarning` (`bulk_signup_warning`) column with getter/setter. It's persisted (not recomputed on read) because the value is a snapshot of the day's count at creation time, exactly like `namesakeCount`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 compute and set the flag before saving, via a new `isBulkSignup(day)` helper that counts owners already carrying the same `registrationDate` and returns `count > 80`. This reuses the same accumulation path as the existing daily-limit rule (`isDailyLimitReached`, which caps at 100), so between 81 and 99 same-day owners the warning is `true`, and `false` otherwise.\n\n4. **Schema SQL** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column. The test profile uses `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so this column is required for the field to persist.\n\nA fresh single create returns `bulkSignupWarning: false`, matching the acceptance test, and the `>80` branch flips it to `true`.",
